"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkdocku=globalThis.webpackChunkdocku||[]).push([[8566],{29816(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"idira","metadata":{"permalink":"/RSA/pam/idira","source":"@site/pam/idira/idira.mdx","title":"Idira Privilege Cloud","description":"Idira Privilege Cloud (Palo Alto Networks, formerly CyberArk Privilege Cloud) is an enterprise privileged access management SaaS platform. It secures credentials, controls access to privileged accounts, and audits access across hybrid and cloud infrastructures.Versions: 8.0.0 P10","date":"2026-10-10T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Idira","permalink":"/RSA/pam/tags/idira"},{"inline":true,"label":"PAM","permalink":"/RSA/pam/tags/pam"}],"readingTime":12.83,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Pradeep Kadambar","title":"Creator","url":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/pradeepkg/","imageURL":"https://github.com/pradeepkg.png","key":"pradeepkg","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"idira","title":"Idira Privilege Cloud","date":"2026-10-10T00:00:00.000Z","authors":"pradeepkg","tags":["Idira","PAM"],"keywords":["Idira","CyberArk","Privilege Cloud","PAM","Password Vault"],"draft":false},"unlisted":false,"lastUpdatedAt":1791650237000,"nextItem":{"title":"Keeper Enterprise","permalink":"/RSA/pam/keeper"}},"content":"import logoPNG from \'@site/static/img/logos/Idira - 180x180.png\';\\nimport ImageWithZoom from \'@site/src/components/ImageWithZoom\';\\n\\n<table>\\n  <tr>\\n    <td width=\\"180\\" class=\\"image-cell\\"><img src={logoPNG} width=\\"180\\" height=\\"180\\"/>Idira Privilege Cloud (Palo Alto Networks, formerly CyberArk Privilege Cloud) is an enterprise privileged access management SaaS platform. It secures credentials, controls access to privileged accounts, and audits access across hybrid and cloud infrastructures.<br /><br />Versions: `8.0.0 P10`</td>\\n  </tr>\\n</table>\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Introduction\\n\\nThis plugin integrates RSA Governance & Lifecycle (G&L) with Idira Privilege Cloud (Palo Alto Networks Idira, formerly CyberArk Privilege Cloud). It retrieves managed credentials for collectors and connectors using Idira Identity OAuth 2.0 client credentials and the Privilege Cloud PVWA REST API.\\n\\nCredentials are not cached; each collection or connector execution fetches the current secret directly from Privilege Cloud, ensuring that credential rotation schedules are respected automatically without requiring manual updates in RSA G&L.\\n\\n## Prerequisites\\n\\nEnsure you have the following before configuring the integration:\\n\\n- Administrative access to Idira Identity Administration and Privilege Cloud Safe management.\\n- An active Idira Privilege Cloud tenant (qualified against version 15.0 or later).\\n- RSA Governance & Lifecycle 8.0.0 Patch 10 or later.\\n- Outbound HTTPS (TCP port 443) network connectivity from the RSA G&L server and any remote agents to both Idira Identity (`https://<tenant-id>.id.cyberark.cloud`) and Privilege Cloud (`https://<subdomain>.privilegecloud.cyberark.cloud`).\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Idira Side Setup\\n\\nConfigure Idira Privilege Cloud for OAuth-based API access by RSA G&L using least-privilege permissions.\\n\\n:::info\\nThis section is provided for reference. Work with your Idira administrator to ensure all configurations comply with enterprise security policies.\\n:::\\n\\n```\\nManage space\\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 Inventory \u2192 Identities \u2192 Users     (Step 2: Service User)\\n\u251c\u2500\u2500 Inventory \u2192 Identities \u2192 Roles     (Step 3: Role Membership)\\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 Policies  \u2192 Safes                  (Step 4: Dedicated Safe)\\n\\nPrivilege Cloud space\\n\u2514\u2500\u2500 Accounts \u2192 Accounts & Requests \u2192 Accounts View (Step 5: Target Credential)\\n```\\n\\n### Step 1: Identify Tenant URLs and Login Suffix\\n\\nThe plugin requires two tenant endpoints:\\n\\n| G&L Configuration Field | Description | Format Example |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **Identity Tenant URL** | Idira Identity OAuth 2.0 token endpoint host | `https://<tenant-id>.id.cyberark.cloud` |\\n| **Privilege Cloud URL** | Privilege Cloud PVWA REST API host | `https://<subdomain>.privilegecloud.cyberark.cloud` |\\n\\n:::tip Discovering Tenant URLs\\nBoth URLs can be determined using Idira platform discovery:\\n```bash\\ncurl -s https://platform-discovery.cyberark.cloud/api/v2/services/subdomain/<subdomain>\\n```\\nExtract `identity_administration.api` for the **Identity Tenant URL** and `pcloud.api` (without a trailing slash) for the **Privilege Cloud URL**.\\n:::\\n\\nNote your **login suffix** (for example, `cyberark.cloud.12345`), visible under your username in the profile menu. The service user uses this same suffix.\\n\\n### Step 2: Create the Service User\\n\\n1. Navigate to **Manage \u2192 Inventory \u2192 Identities \u2192 Users** and click **Add User**.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/idira-02-users-list.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n2. Complete the **Account** details:\\n   - **Login name**: Enter a dedicated name (for example, `svc_gl` or `svc_rsa_gl_pam`).\\n   - **Suffix**: Ensure your tenant login suffix is selected. The full `login@suffix` becomes the **Client ID**.\\n   - **Email address**: Enter a monitored distribution list or team mailbox.\\n   - **Display name**: For example, `RSA G&L Service Account`.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/idira-03-add-user-form-1.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n3. Set **Password Type** to **Generated**. Click **Copy** and store this generated password in your secure vault. This value becomes the **Client Secret**.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/idira-03-add-user-form-2.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n4. Under **Status**, select **Is OAuth confidential client**. Idira automatically enables **Is service user** and **Password never expires**.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/idira-03-add-user-form-3.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n5. Click **Create User**.\\n\\nRecord the generated credentials for RSA G&L:\\n- **Client ID**: The full login name (for example, `svc_gl@cyberark.cloud.12345`)\\n- **Client Secret**: The generated password\\n\\n### Step 3: Assign the Privilege Cloud Users Role\\n\\n1. In the **Manage** space, navigate to **Inventory \u2192 Identities \u2192 Roles**.\\n2. Search for `Privilege Cloud Users`.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/idira-06-roles-list.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n3. Open **Privilege Cloud Users**, select the **Members** tab, click **Add**, select the service user, and click **Save**.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/idira-07-role-add-member.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n\\n:::caution Least Privilege\\nDo not assign the service user to *Privilege Cloud Administrators*, *Safe Managers*, or any other administrative role. The service user requires member access to *Privilege Cloud Users* only.\\n:::\\n\\n### Step 4: Create a Dedicated Safe and Assign Permissions\\n\\nTo isolate secrets accessed by RSA G&L, create a dedicated Safe.\\n\\n1. Navigate to **Manage \u2192 Policies \u2192 Safes** and click **Create Safe**.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/idira-08-add-safe.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n2. **Define Safe Properties**: Enter a Safe Name (for example, `RSA-GL-Integration`) and a description. Leave dual-control disabled.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/idira-08-add-safe-1.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n3. **Select Safe Members**: Search for and select the service user created in Step 2.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/idira-08-add-safe-2.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n4. **Set Permissions**: Under **Access**, select **List accounts** and **Retrieve accounts**. Clear **Use accounts**.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/idira-10-safe-permissions.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n5. Click **Create Safe**.\\n\\n| Permission | Required | Usage |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| **List accounts** | Yes | Account lookup and connectivity validation |\\n| **Retrieve accounts** | Yes | Decrypting and retrieving account passwords |\\n| **Use accounts** | No | Interactive sessions (not required by RSA G&L) |\\n\\n### Step 5: Store Target Credentials in Privilege Cloud\\n\\nEnsure the target account platform is active before adding credentials:\\n1. Navigate to **Manage \u2192 Policies \u2192 Account platforms**. Select the platform group (for example, **Cloud Service**) and activate the required platform if needed.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/idira-11-account-platforms.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p><p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/idira-11-platforms-activate.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n2. Switch to the **Privilege Cloud** space (**Navigate \u22ee\u22ee \u2192 Privilege Cloud**), go to **Accounts \u2192 Accounts & Requests \u2192 Accounts View**, and click **Add account**.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/idira-12-accounts.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n3. Select the **System type** (for example, Cloud Service), the **Platform**, and select the dedicated Safe (`RSA-GL-Integration`).<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/idira-12-account-1.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p><p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/idira-12-account-2.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p><p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/idira-12-account-3.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n4. Enter the account properties:\\n   - **Username**: The account login or application client ID.\\n   - **Address**: Target host, domain, or directory ID (if applicable).\\n   - **Password**: The secret or password value.\\n   - **Customize account name**: Enter a unique name (for example, `Entra-RSA-GL-Collector`).<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/idira-12-account-4.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n5. Click **Add**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Installation and Configuration in G&L\\n\\n:::note\\nRequires RSA Governance and Lifecycle 8.0.0 Patch 10 or later.\\n:::\\n\\nComplete the [Idira Side Setup](#idira-side-setup) before proceeding. You will need the **Privilege Cloud URL**, **Identity Tenant URL**, **Client ID**, and **Client Secret**.\\n\\n### Step 1: Install the Plugin\\n\\n1. Log in to the RSA G&L UI as a **System Administrator**.\\n2. Navigate to **Admin \u2192 System \u2192 Password Vault**.\\n3. Under **Vault Types**, click **Upload New Vault Type**.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/GL_01.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n4. In the dialog, enter the details:\\n   - **Vault Type Name**: `Idira-Privilege-Cloud`\\n   - **Description**: `Retrieves credentials from Idira Privilege Cloud using OAuth 2.0 client credentials.`\\n   - **JAR File**: Click **Choose File** and select the distribution JAR (`idira-privilegecloud-plugin-dist-1.0-<timestamp>.jar`).<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/GL_02.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n5. Click **OK**. **Idira-Privilege-Cloud** appears in the Vault Types list.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/GL_03.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n\\n:::note\\nAfter uploading a new vault type, restart all remote G&L agents to ensure they load the plugin.\\n:::\\n\\n### Step 2: Configure the Password Vault\\n\\n1. In the **Password Vault Configuration** section, select **Idira-Privilege-Cloud** from the dropdown and click **+ Create**.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/GL_04.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n2. Complete the configuration form:\\n\\n   | Field | Description | Example |\\n   |---|---|---|\\n   | **Privilege Cloud URL** | Base URL of your Privilege Cloud PVWA instance | `https://<subdomain>.privilegecloud.cyberark.cloud` |\\n   | **Identity Tenant URL** | Base URL of your Idira Identity Administration tenant | `https://<tenant-id>.id.cyberark.cloud` |\\n   | **Client ID** | Service user login identifier including suffix | `svc_gl@cyberark.cloud.12345` |\\n   | **Client Secret** | Service user generated secret key (masked) | `\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022\u2022` |\\n\\n3. Click **Test Connection**. A **Connection successful** status confirms token retrieval and API communication.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/GL_05.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n4. Click **OK** to save. The saved configuration displays **Edit** and **Test Connection** options.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/GL_06.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n\\n### Step 3: Create Vault Profiles\\n\\nA vault profile maps a specific collector or connector credential to an account stored in an Idira Safe. Create one profile per managed account.\\n\\n1. Navigate to the **Password Vault Profiles** section and click **+ Create Profile**.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/GL_07.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n2. Fill in the profile fields:\\n\\n   | Field | Description | Example |\\n   |---|---|---|\\n   | **Name** | Display name for this profile | `Entra RSA G&L Collector` |\\n   | **Description** | Optional description | `Microsoft Entra client secret` |\\n   | **Safe Name** | Safe where the credential is stored | `RSA-GL-Integration` |\\n   | **Account Name** | Account Name or Username in Privilege Cloud | `Entra-RSA-GL-Collector` |\\n\\n3. Click **Test Profile**. A **Credential retrieved successfully** response confirms the account was found and its password decrypted.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/GL_07.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n4. Click **OK** to save. The profile appears in the list with the identifier formatted as `Safe Name (Account Name)`.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/GL_08.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n\\n### Step 4: Use the Profile in a Collector or Connector\\n\\nIn the collector or connector configuration, keep non-sensitive parameters static and reference the vault profile for the secret:\\n\\n1. Open the collector configuration (for example, *Microsoft Entra ID*).\\n2. Enter the static **Username** or Application ID.\\n3. For **Password**, select **Use Password Vault \u2192 Existing Profile** and choose your profile (`Entra RSA G&L Collector`).<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/GL_09.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n4. Click **Test**, then click **Save**.\\n\\nRSA G&L retrieves the current password from Idira Privilege Cloud each time the collector or connector runs.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Known Limitations\\n\\n- **Client Credentials Only**: The plugin uses the OAuth 2.0 client credentials grant for machine-to-machine authentication. User interactive authentication and authorization code grants are not supported.\\n- **Dual-Control Safes Not Supported**: Safes requiring access approval return HTTP 403. G&L automated background retrieval cannot submit interactive access requests.\\n- **No Check-in / Checkout Locking**: Passwords are retrieved on demand without exclusive checkout. Do not configure one-time-password or exclusive checkout policies for credentials used by RSA G&L.\\n- **Exact Account Matching**: Privilege Cloud account searches return fuzzy matches. The plugin enforces an exact match against the Account Name first, then the Username within the specified Safe.\\n- **HTTPS Enforced**: All endpoints require HTTPS. HTTP redirects are disabled to safeguard credentials.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## Troubleshooting\\n\\n#### Authentication and Token Errors (HTTP 400)\\n\\n**Error Message:**\\n> `Idira Identity token exchange failed with status: 400 (access_denied: invalid client creds or client not allowed)`\\n\\n- **Root Cause**: Invalid Client ID or Client Secret, or the service user is not configured as an OAuth confidential client.\\n- **Resolution**:\\n  1. Verify the **Client ID** includes the full login suffix (for example, `svc_gl@cyberark.cloud.12345`).\\n  2. Confirm **Is OAuth confidential client** is selected for the service user in Idira Identity Administration.\\n  3. Reset the service user password in Idira Identity Administration with *Require password change at next login* cleared, update the secret in RSA G&L, and test the connection.\\n\\n#### API Authorization and Role Membership (HTTP 401)\\n\\n**Error Message:**\\n> `Idira Accounts API returned error status: 401`\\n\\n- **Root Cause**: The service user authenticated successfully but is not authorized to query Privilege Cloud APIs, or the Privilege Cloud URL is incorrect.\\n- **Resolution**:\\n  1. Confirm the service user is a member of the **Privilege Cloud Users** role.\\n  2. Verify the **Privilege Cloud URL** matches `https://<subdomain>.privilegecloud.cyberark.cloud` without additional path elements such as `/PasswordVault`.\\n\\n#### Account Lookup and Resolution\\n\\n**Error Message:**\\n> `No account was found for the provided SafeName and AccountName`\\n\\n- **Root Cause**: The Safe Name or Account Name does not match any entry in Privilege Cloud, or the service user lacks list permissions on the Safe.\\n- **Resolution**:\\n  1. Verify the **Safe Name** matches the Safe in Privilege Cloud exactly (case-insensitive).\\n  2. Confirm the **Account Name** matches either the custom account name or the account username.\\n  3. Ensure the service user has **List accounts** permissions on the Safe.\\n\\n**Error Message:**\\n> `Multiple accounts found for the provided SafeName and AccountName`\\n\\n- **Root Cause**: More than one account in the Safe has the same username or account name.\\n- **Resolution**:\\n  1. In Privilege Cloud, assign a unique custom account name (for example, `Entra-RSA-GL-Collector`).\\n  2. Update the RSA G&L Vault Profile **Account Name** to specify that unique name.\\n\\n#### Password Retrieval and Safe Permissions (HTTP 403 / 404)\\n\\n**Error Message:**\\n> `Idira denied immediate password retrieval (403)`\\n\\n- **Root Cause**: The service user lacks retrieve permissions, or the Safe has dual-control workflow active.\\n- **Resolution**:\\n  1. Verify the service user has **Retrieve accounts** permission on the Safe.\\n  2. Confirm dual-control approval is disabled on the Safe.\\n\\n**Error Message:**\\n> `Idira account was not found during password retrieval (404)`\\n\\n- **Root Cause**: The account was deleted or modified in Privilege Cloud between search lookup and password retrieval.\\n- **Resolution**:\\n  1. Confirm the account exists in Privilege Cloud.\\n  2. Retest the profile in RSA G&L.\\n\\n#### Network and TLS Connectivity\\n\\n**Error Message:**\\n> `Connectivity or parsing error during Idira Identity token exchange` or `Connectivity error with Idira Privilege Cloud API`\\n\\n- **Root Cause**: Network egress restrictions, firewall blocking, proxy interference, or TLS inspection preventing HTTPS communication.\\n- **Resolution**:\\n  1. Ensure outbound HTTPS (port 443) is open from the RSA G&L server and remote agents to `*.id.cyberark.cloud` and `*.privilegecloud.cyberark.cloud`.\\n  2. Ensure endpoint URLs begin with `https://`.\\n  3. Confirm intermediate TLS inspection proxies do not intercept or terminate connections without presenting trusted certificates.\\n\\n#### Profile Configuration Mismatch\\n\\n**Error Message:**\\n> `Password vault profile is not an Idira Privilege Cloud profile`\\n\\n- **Root Cause**: The profile was created under a different vault type.\\n- **Resolution**:\\n  1. Create a new profile under the **Idira-Privilege-Cloud** vault configuration.\\n  2. Assign the new profile to the target collector or connector.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## References\\n\\n**Idira Identity Documentation**\\n- [Add service users](https://docs.cyberark.com/identity-administration/latest/en/content/ispss/ispss-add-service-user.htm): Creating service users with OAuth confidential client authentication.\\n- [API token authentication for Idira Identity](https://api-docs.cyberark.com/create-api-token/docs/create-api-token): OAuth 2.0 `/oauth2/platformtoken` endpoint reference.\\n\\n**Idira Privilege Cloud Documentation**\\n- [Manage Safe members](https://docs.cyberark.com/privilege-cloud-shared-services/latest/en/content/privilege%20cloud/privcloud-manage-safe-members.htm): Safe permissions and member assignment.\\n- [Add accounts](https://docs.cyberark.com/privilege-cloud-shared-services/latest/en/content/privilege%20cloud/privcloud-onboard.htm): Creating and managing privileged accounts.\\n- [Microsoft Azure Application Keys platform](https://docs.cyberark.com/privilege-cloud-shared-services/latest/en/content/pasimp/microsoftazureapplicationkeys.htm): Platform configuration for application secrets.\\n- [Privilege Cloud REST APIs overview](https://docs.cyberark.com/privilege-cloud-shared-services/latest/en/content/webservices/implementing%20privileged%20account%20security%20web%20services%20.htm): PVWA REST API specifications.\\n- [Get accounts API](https://docs.cyberark.com/privilege-cloud-shared-services/latest/en/content/sdk/getaccounts.htm): `GET /API/Accounts` search and filter parameters.\\n- [Get password value API](https://docs.cyberark.com/privilege-cloud-shared-services/latest/en/content/webservices/getpasswordvaluev10.htm): `POST /API/Accounts/{id}/Password/Retrieve` retrieval specifications.\\n\\n**Industry Standards**\\n- [RFC 6749 Section 4.4: Client Credentials Grant](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749#section-4.4): OAuth 2.0 framework specification."},{"id":"keeper","metadata":{"permalink":"/RSA/pam/keeper","source":"@site/pam/keeper/keeper.mdx","title":"Keeper Enterprise","description":"Keeper Enterprise is a zero-knowledge, end-to-end encrypted secrets management platform with privileged access management capabilities. It provides secure credential vaulting, just-in-time access, and password rotation for enterprise infrastructure across cloud, on-premises, and hybrid environments.Versions: 8.0.0 P10","date":"2026-04-01T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"Keeper","permalink":"/RSA/pam/tags/keeper"},{"inline":true,"label":"PAM","permalink":"/RSA/pam/tags/pam"}],"readingTime":8.03,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Pradeep Kadambar","title":"Creator","url":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/pradeepkg/","imageURL":"https://github.com/pradeepkg.png","key":"pradeepkg","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"keeper","title":"Keeper Enterprise","date":"2026-04-01T00:00:00.000Z","authors":"pradeepkg","tags":["Keeper","PAM"],"keywords":["Keeper","PAM","KSM","Secrets Manager"],"draft":false},"unlisted":false,"lastUpdatedAt":1791650237000,"prevItem":{"title":"Idira Privilege Cloud","permalink":"/RSA/pam/idira"},"nextItem":{"title":"BeyondTrust Password Safe","permalink":"/RSA/pam/beyondtrust"}},"content":"import logoPNG from \'@site/static/img/logos/Keeper - 180x180.png\';\\nimport ImageWithZoom from \'@site/src/components/ImageWithZoom\';\\n\\n<table>\\n  <tr>\\n    <td width=\\"180\\" class=\\"image-cell\\"><img src={logoPNG} width=\\"180\\" height=\\"180\\"/>Keeper Enterprise is a zero-knowledge, end-to-end encrypted secrets management platform with privileged access management capabilities. It provides secure credential vaulting, just-in-time access, and password rotation for enterprise infrastructure across cloud, on-premises, and hybrid environments.<br /><br />Versions: `8.0.0 P10`</td>\\n  </tr>\\n</table>\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Introduction\\n\\nThis plugin integrates RSA Governance and Lifecycle (G&L) with Keeper Enterprise using the Keeper Secrets Manager (KSM) Java SDK. It retrieves credentials for use by collectors and connectors. Credentials are not cached; each retrieval fetches the current password directly from Keeper. All HTTPS transport and end-to-end encryption are handled by the KSM SDK.\\n\\n## Prerequisites\\n\\nEnsure you have the following:\\n\\n- Administrative access to your Keeper Enterprise vault.\\n- The Keeper Secrets Manager CLI installed on a machine used to initialize credentials.\\n- A list of managed accounts that RSA G&L will access.\\n\\n## Keeper Side Setup\\n\\nThis section describes how to configure Keeper Enterprise for SDK-based access by RSA G&L.\\n\\n:::info\\nThis section is provided for reference only. Contact your Keeper administrator to configure Keeper Enterprise in line with your organization\'s security requirements.\\n:::\\n\\n### Step 1: Create a Shared Folder\\n\\nKeeper Secrets Manager applications can only access Shared Folders. Regular folders are not visible to the SDK.\\n\\n:::warning\\nUse **Create New > Shared Folder** (identified by a two-person icon). If you use **Create New > Folder** (plain folder), the KSM application will not see it. If the folder does not appear in Secrets Manager, delete it and recreate it as a Shared Folder.\\n:::\\n\\n1. Log in to **Keeper Vault** at `keepersecurity.com`.\\n2. Click **Create New** > **Shared Folder**.\\n3. Complete the following fields and click **Create**.\\n\\n   | Field | Value |\\n   |---|---|\\n   | **Folder Name** | A descriptive name, for example `AD Credentials` |\\n   | **User Permissions** | `No User Permissions` |\\n   | **Record Permissions** | `View Only` |\\n\\n### Step 2: Create a Login Record\\n\\n1. In **My Vault**, open the shared folder, click the folder menu **(...)**, and select **New Record**.\\n2. Select record type **Login**.\\n3. Complete the following fields and click **Save**.\\n\\n   | Field | Value |\\n   |---|---|\\n   | **Title** | A descriptive name for the account, for example `AD Service Account` |\\n   | **Login** | The username |\\n   | **Password** | The credential password |\\n\\n### Step 3: Note the Record UID\\n\\nThe Record UID is the permanent identifier used by the plugin to locate a record. It does not change if the record is renamed or moved.\\n\\n1. Open the record in Keeper Vault.\\n2. Click the record menu **(...)** at the top-right of the record panel and select **Record Info**.\\n3. Copy the **UID**, a 22-character Base64 string, for example `YSq-xxLdgsO2RJbz46EKkA`.\\n\\nThis value is entered in the G&L vault profile as the **Record UID**.\\n\\n### Step 4: Create a KSM Application\\n\\n1. In Keeper Vault, click **Secrets Manager** in the left sidebar.\\n2. Click **Create Application**, enter a name (for example `GL-PAM`), and click **Save**.\\n3. With the application selected, click the **Folders and Records** tab.\\n4. Search for and select your shared folder. It is added with **Read Only** access.\\n5. Click **Save**. The **Records** column should show a count greater than zero.\\n\\n### Step 5: Generate an Access Token and Initialize Credentials\\n\\n:::warning\\nOne-time access tokens cannot be retrieved after generation. Copy the token before closing this dialog.\\n:::\\n\\n1. With the application selected, click the **Devices** tab and click **Add Device**.\\n2. Complete the following fields.\\n\\n   | Field | Value |\\n   |---|---|\\n   | **Device Name** | A descriptive name, for example `GL-PAM-Plugin` |\\n   | **Method** | `One-Time Access Token` |\\n   | **Lock external WAN IP** | Leave unchecked |\\n\\n3. Click **Generate Access Token** and copy the token. The format is `US:xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx`.\\n\\n4. On any machine with the KSM CLI installed, run the following to exchange the token for permanent credentials.\\n\\n   ```bash\\n   # Install the CLI (one-time setup)\\n   pip install keeper-secrets-manager-cli\\n\\n   # Exchange the token to create keeper.ini in your home directory\\n   ksm profile init --token <YOUR_ONE_TIME_TOKEN>\\n   ```\\n\\n5. Open the generated `keeper.ini` file (`%USERPROFILE%\\\\keeper.ini` on Windows, `~/keeper.ini` on Linux/macOS).\\n\\n   ```ini\\n   [_default]\\n   clientid          = <base64 string>\\n   privatekey        = <base64 string>\\n   appkey            = <base64 string>\\n   hostname          = keepersecurity.com\\n   appownerpublickey = <base64 string>\\n   serverpublickeyid = 10\\n   ```\\n\\n   Each value maps directly to a field in the G&L vault configuration in the next section.\\n\\n## Installation and Configuration in G&L\\n\\n:::note\\nRequires RSA Governance and Lifecycle 8.0.0 Patch 10 or later.\\n:::\\n\\nComplete the [Keeper Side Setup](#keeper-side-setup) section before proceeding. You will need the values from the `keeper.ini` file generated in Step 5.\\n\\n### Step 1: Install the Plugin\\n\\n1. Log in to the RSA G&L UI as a **System Administrator**.\\n2. Open the **Admin** menu and click **System**.\\n3. Click the **Password Vault** tab.\\n4. Scroll to the **Vault Types** section and click **Upload New Vault Type**.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/01.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n5. Enter a **Vault Type Name** (for example, `Keeper-Enterprise`) and an optional description. Click **Choose File**, select the distribution JAR (`keeper-enterprise-plugin-dist-1.0-<timestamp>.jar`), and click **OK**.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/02.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n\\n   :::note\\n   After uploading, restart all remote G&L agents to load the new vault type.\\n   :::\\n\\n6. After the upload completes, **Keeper-Enterprise** appears in the Vault Types list.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/03.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n\\n### Step 2: Configure the Password Vault\\n\\n1. Scroll to the **Password Vault Configuration** section. Select **Keeper-Enterprise** from the dropdown and click **+ Create**.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/04.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n2. Copy the values from `keeper.ini` into the corresponding fields, click **Test Connection**, and click **OK** to save.\\n\\n   | G&L Field | `keeper.ini` Key | Description |\\n   |---|---|---|\\n   | **Hostname** | `hostname` | Keeper server hostname. Varies by region: `keepersecurity.com` (US), `keepersecurity.eu` (EU), `keepersecurity.com.au` (AU), `keepersecurity.jp` (JP), `keepersecurity.ca` (CA) |\\n   | **Client ID** | `clientid` | Base64-encoded application identity |\\n   | **Private Key** | `privatekey` | Base64-encoded ECC private key (masked) |\\n   | **App Key** | `appkey` | Base64-encoded application encryption key (masked) |\\n   | **App Owner Public Key** | `appownerpublickey` | Optional but recommended |\\n   | **Server Public Key ID** | `serverpublickeyid` | Defaults to `10` |\\n\\n   <p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/05.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n\\n3. The saved configuration shows **Edit**, **Test Connection**, and **Delete** options. Use **Test Connection** at any time to verify connectivity.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/06.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n\\n### Step 3: Create Vault Profiles\\n\\nA vault profile maps a G&L managed account to a specific record in Keeper by its UID. Create one profile per account.\\n\\n1. Navigate to the **Password Vault Profiles** section and click **+ Create Profile**.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/07.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n2. Complete the following fields and click **Test Profile**. A `Credential retrieved successfully` response confirms the UID is valid and the password is accessible. Click **OK** to save.\\n\\n   | Field | Description | Example |\\n   |---|---|---|\\n   | **Name** | Display name for this profile | `Active Directory` |\\n   | **Description** | Optional description | `AD service account` |\\n   | **Record UID** | The 22-character Base64 UID copied in [Step 3](#step-3-note-the-record-uid) of the Keeper Side Setup | `YSq-xxLdgsO2RJbz46EKkA` |\\n\\n   <p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/08.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n\\n3. The profile appears in the list. The **Identifier** column shows the Record UID.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/09.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n4. Repeat for each account that requires Keeper password retrieval.\\n5. In G&L, open the collector or connector configuration, locate **Use Password Vault**, select the profile, click **Test**, and save.\\n\\n## Known Limitations\\n\\n- **Records are accessed by UID, not by title.** Record UIDs are permanent and do not change if a record is renamed or moved. If a record is deleted and recreated, its UID changes and the vault profile must be updated.\\n\\n- **The KSM application must have access to the record\'s Shared Folder.** If the record is moved out of the shared folder that the KSM application can access, credential retrieval will fail. Verify folder membership in Keeper Vault under **Secrets Manager** > application > **Folders and Records**.\\n\\n## Troubleshooting\\n\\n#### Connection Errors\\n\\n- **Root Cause**: Hostname region mismatch, incorrect vault configuration values from `keeper.ini`, or deactivated KSM application.\\n- **Resolution**:\\n  1. Verify that all six vault configuration fields match the values in `keeper.ini` exactly.\\n  2. Confirm the **Hostname** matches your Keeper region (for example, `keepersecurity.com` or `keepersecurity.eu`).\\n  3. Verify that the KSM application is active and has not been deleted in Keeper Vault.\\n\\n#### Record Not Found\\n\\n- **Root Cause**: Invalid Record UID in the vault profile, or the record resides outside the KSM application shared folder.\\n- **Resolution**:\\n  1. Confirm the **Record UID** in the vault profile matches the 22-character UID in Keeper.\\n  2. Verify the record is inside a Shared Folder that the KSM application has permission to access.\\n  3. In Keeper Vault, navigate to **Secrets Manager \u2192 Folders and Records** and verify the folder is listed with a record count greater than zero.\\n\\n#### Access Denied Errors\\n\\n- **Root Cause**: Insufficient permissions on the shared folder or the target record was moved or deleted.\\n- **Resolution**:\\n  1. Verify the KSM application has **Read Only** access granted to the shared folder.\\n  2. Confirm the shared folder contains the target record and has not been removed.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## References\\n\\n**Keeper Security Documentation**\\n- [Keeper Secrets Manager Overview](https://docs.keeper.io/en/v/secrets-manager/secrets-manager/overview): Architecture, zero-knowledge encryption, and access controls.\\n- [Keeper Secrets Manager CLI](https://docs.keeper.io/en/v/secrets-manager/secrets-manager/secrets-manager-command-line-interface): Initializing profiles and exchanging one-time access tokens.\\n- [Keeper Secrets Manager Java SDK](https://docs.keeper.io/en/v/secrets-manager/secrets-manager/developer-sdk-library/java-sdk): Record retrieval methods and caching controls.\\n- [Keeper Security Regions and URLs](https://docs.keeper.io/en/v/secrets-manager/secrets-manager/overview#regional-endpoints): Regional API endpoints and hostnames."},{"id":"beyondtrust","metadata":{"permalink":"/RSA/pam/beyondtrust","source":"@site/pam/beyondtrust/beyondtrust.mdx","title":"BeyondTrust Password Safe","description":"BeyondTrust Password Safe is an enterprise solution for privileged account, credential, and secrets management. It automates password rotation, enforces strong security policies, monitors and audits privileged sessions, and manages access for human and machine identities across traditional, cloud, and DevOps environments.Versions: 8.0.0 P10","date":"2026-03-06T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":true,"label":"BeyondTrust","permalink":"/RSA/pam/tags/beyond-trust"},{"inline":true,"label":"PAM","permalink":"/RSA/pam/tags/pam"}],"readingTime":8.38,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Pradeep Kadambar","title":"Creator","url":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/pradeepkg/","imageURL":"https://github.com/pradeepkg.png","key":"pradeepkg","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"slug":"beyondtrust","title":"BeyondTrust Password Safe","date":"2026-03-06T00:00:00.000Z","authors":"pradeepkg","tags":["BeyondTrust","PAM"],"keywords":["BeyondTrust","PAM"],"draft":false},"unlisted":false,"lastUpdatedAt":1791650237000,"prevItem":{"title":"Keeper Enterprise","permalink":"/RSA/pam/keeper"}},"content":"import logoPNG from \'@site/static/img/logos/BeyondTrust - 180x180.png\';\\nimport ImageWithZoom from \'@site/src/components/ImageWithZoom\';\\n\\n<table>\\n  <tr>\\n    <td width=\\"180\\" class=\\"image-cell\\"><img src={logoPNG} width=\\"180\\" height=\\"180\\"/>BeyondTrust Password Safe is an enterprise solution for privileged account, credential, and secrets management. It automates password rotation, enforces strong security policies, monitors and audits privileged sessions, and manages access for human and machine identities across traditional, cloud, and DevOps environments.<br /><br />Versions: `8.0.0 P10`</td>\\n  </tr>\\n</table>\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Introduction\\n\\nThis plugin integrates RSA Governance and Lifecycle (G&L) with BeyondTrust Password Safe using the BeyondTrust REST API v3 and OAuth 2.0 authentication. It retrieves credentials for use by collectors and connectors. Credentials are not cached; each retrieval fetches the current password directly from BeyondTrust, so password rotation policies are respected automatically.\\n\\n## Prerequisites\\n\\nBefore you begin, ensure you have the following:\\n\\n- Administrative access to your BeyondTrust Password Safe instance.\\n- A list of managed accounts that RSA G&L will access.\\n- The IP address or CIDR range of the RSA G&L instance for API access configuration.\\n\\n## Application Setup\\n\\nThis section describes how to configure BeyondTrust Password Safe for OAuth-based API access by RSA G&L.\\n\\n:::info\\nThis section is provided for reference only. Contact your BeyondTrust administrator to configure BeyondTrust Password Safe in line with your organization\'s security requirements.\\n:::\\n\\n### Step 1: Create a User Group and Smart Group\\n1. **Create a User Group:**\\n   1. Navigate to **Configuration** > **Role Based Access** > **User Management**.\\n   2. Click the **Groups** tab and select **Create New Group** > **Create a New Group**.\\n   3. Enter `API Access` as the **Group Name**, provide a **Description**, and click **Create Group**.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/app-2.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n\\n2. **Create a Smart Group:**\\n   1. Navigate to **Managed Accounts**.\\n   2. Select the accounts RSA G&L will access.\\n   3. Click **Add to Smart Group**.\\n   4. In the **Add To Manual Smart Group** dialog, enter `G&L` in the **Smart Group (manual)** field.\\n   5. Click **Add As New Option**.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/app-4.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n   6. Set **Category** to **Managed Accounts**, provide an optional **Description**, and click **Add Selected Accounts To Smart Group**.\\n\\n### Step 2: Configure API Access and Permissions\\n1. **Configure an API Access Policy:**\\n   1. Go to **Configuration** > **General** > **API Registrations**.\\n   2. Click **Create API Registration** and select **API Access Policy**.\\n   3. Fill in the registration details, including the **Access Token Duration**.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/app-1.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n   4. Click **Add Authentication Rule**, select **CIDR** and **IP Rule**, and enter the CIDR range for your RSA G&L instance.\\n   5. Click **Create Rule**.\\n\\n2. **Assign Permissions:**\\n   1. Open the `API Access` group and select the **Features** tab.\\n   2. Select **All Features**, then search for and select **Password Safe Account Management** and **Password Safe System Management**.\\n   3. Click **Assign Permissions** and grant **Read Only** access.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/app-3.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n\\n### Step 3: Create an Access Policy\\n1. Go to **Configuration** > **Privileged Access Management Policies** > **Access Policies**.\\n2. Click **Create Policy**.\\n3. Enter `G&L API Auto Approve Policy` as the **Access Policy Name**, provide a **Description**, and click **Create Policy**.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/app-8.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n4. On the **Schedule** tab, click **Create Schedule**.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/app-9.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n5. Enable **All Day** and verify the **Recurrence** settings.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/app-6.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n6. Under **Policy Types**, select **View Password**, **Auto Approve**, and **API Only Access**.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/app-7.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n7. Click **Create Schedule**.\\n\\n### Step 4: Assign Smart Group Roles\\n1. Go to **User Management** > **Groups** > `API Access`.\\n2. Click the **Smart Groups** tab, find the `G&L` smart group, and select **Edit Password Safe Roles**.\\n3. Select the **Requestor** role.\\n4. For **Access Policy**, select `G&L API Auto Approve Policy`.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/app-10.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n5. Click **Save Roles**.\\n\\n### Step 5: Create an API User\\n1. Go to **Configuration** > **Role Based Access** > **User Management**.\\n2. Click the **Users** tab and select **Add an Application User**.\\n3. Enter `svc_gl_pam` as the **Username**.\\n4. Under **API Access Policy**, select the policy you created.\\n5. Copy and store the **Client ID** and **Client Secret**. These are required for the G&L configuration.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/app-11.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n6. Click **Create User**.\\n7. Assign the user to the `API Access` group.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/app-12.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n\\n### Step 6: Enable API Access on Managed Accounts\\n1. Go to **Managed Accounts**.\\n2. For each account, set **Enable API Access** to **Yes** in the **Account Settings**.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/app-13.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n\\n## Installation and Configuration in G&L\\n\\n:::note\\nRequires RSA Governance and Lifecycle 8.0.0 Patch 10 or later.\\n:::\\n\\nComplete the [Application Setup](#application-setup) section before proceeding. You will need the **Client ID** and **Client Secret** from Step 5.\\n\\n### Step 1: Install the Plugin\\n1. Log in to the RSA G&L UI as a **System Administrator**.\\n2. Open the **Admin** menu and click **System**.\\n3. Click the **Password Vault** tab.\\n4. Scroll to the **Vault Types** section and click **Upload New Vault Type**.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/01.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n5. Enter a **Vault Type Name** (for example, `BeyondTrust-Password-Safe`) and an optional description. Click **Choose File**, select the distribution JAR (`beyondtrust-password-safe-plugin-dist-1.0-<timestamp>.jar`), and click **OK**.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/02.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n\\n   :::note\\n   After uploading, restart all remote G&L agents to load the new vault type.\\n   :::\\n\\n6. After the upload completes, **BeyondTrust Password Safe** appears in the Vault Types list.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/03.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n\\n### Step 2: Configure the Password Vault\\n1. Scroll to the **Password Vault Configuration** section. Select **BeyondTrust Password Safe** from the dropdown and click **+ Create**.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/04.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n2. Complete the following fields, click **Test Connection**, and click **OK** to save.\\n\\n   | Field | Description |\\n   |---|---|\\n   | **Base URL** | Base URL of your BeyondTrust Password Safe instance (for example, `https://instance.company.com`) |\\n   | **Client ID** | OAuth 2.0 Client ID from the registered BeyondTrust application |\\n   | **Client Secret** | OAuth 2.0 Client Secret from the registered BeyondTrust application (masked) |\\n\\n   <p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/05.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n\\n3. The saved configuration shows **Edit**, **Test Connection**, and **Delete** options. Use **Test Connection** at any time to verify connectivity.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/06.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n\\n### Step 3: Create Vault Profiles\\nA vault profile maps a G&L managed account to a Managed System and Account in BeyondTrust. Create one profile per account.\\n\\n1. Navigate to the **Password Vault Profiles** section and click **+ Create Profile**.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/07.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n2. Complete the following fields and click **Test Profile**. A `Credential retrieved successfully` response confirms the account is reachable in BeyondTrust. Click **OK** to save.\\n\\n   | Field | Description | Example |\\n   |---|---|---|\\n   | **Name** | Display name for this profile | `Oracle DB Admin` |\\n   | **Description** | Optional description | `Oracle production DBA account` |\\n   | **System Name** | Managed System name exactly as it appears in BeyondTrust Password Safe | `PROD-DB-01` |\\n   | **Account Name** | Managed Account name exactly as it appears in BeyondTrust Password Safe | `oracle_dba` |\\n\\n   <p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/08.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n\\n3. The profile appears in the list. The **Identifier** column shows `System Name (Account Name)`.<p><ImageWithZoom src={require(\'./img/09.png\').default} align=\\"left\\"/></p>\\n4. Repeat for each account that requires BeyondTrust password retrieval.\\n5. In G&L, open the collector or connector configuration, locate **Use Password Vault**, select the profile, click **Test**, and save.\\n\\n## Known Limitations\\n- **Fixed request duration.** Each credential checkout is valid for 1 minute. If a checkout is still active when a new request is submitted for the same account, BeyondTrust returns a 409 Conflict error. Wait for the active request to expire and retry, or manually check in the request from the BeyondTrust Password Safe UI.\\n- **Double-quote characters in passwords.** Double-quote characters are removed from retrieved passwords. If an account password contains double quotes, update the password in BeyondTrust to use a different special character.\\n\\n## Troubleshooting\\n\\n#### API Authentication Errors\\n\\n- **Root Cause**: Invalid Client ID or Client Secret, unassigned API user group, or unauthorized source IP address.\\n- **Resolution**:\\n  1. Verify that the Client ID and Client Secret match the application user credentials.\\n  2. Confirm that the API user is assigned to the `API Access` user group.\\n  3. Confirm that the API access policy CIDR range includes the IP address of the RSA G&L instance and all remote agents.\\n\\n#### Unable to Retrieve Passwords\\n\\n- **Root Cause**: Missing role assignment on the smart group, approval schedule requirement, or API access disabled on accounts.\\n- **Resolution**:\\n  1. Confirm that the `Requestor` role is assigned to the `G&L` smart group.\\n  2. Verify that the access policy is configured to auto-approve requests across all hours.\\n  3. Confirm that API access is enabled on the managed accounts in BeyondTrust Account Settings.\\n\\n#### Access Denied Errors\\n\\n- **Root Cause**: Missing feature permissions for the user group.\\n- **Resolution**:\\n  1. Verify that the `API Access` user group has **Read Only** permissions granted for both **Password Safe Account Management** and **Password Safe System Management**.\\n\\n## References\\n\\n**BeyondTrust Documentation**\\n- [BeyondTrust Password Safe REST API Guide](https://www.beyondtrust.com/docs/password-safe/ps-on-prem/api/api-v3/index.htm): REST API v3 authentication, endpoints, and credentials retrieval.\\n- [BeyondTrust Password Safe Administration](https://www.beyondtrust.com/docs/password-safe/index.htm): User groups, Smart Groups, and access policy configuration.\\n- [BeyondTrust Password Safe Managed Accounts](https://www.beyondtrust.com/docs/password-safe/ps-on-prem/index.htm): Enabling API access and configuring auto-approve access policies.\\n\\n**Industry Standards**\\n- [RFC 6749 Section 4.4: Client Credentials Grant](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc6749#section-4.4): OAuth 2.0 machine-to-machine authentication specification."}]}}')}}]);